Revision history for SICK

{{$NEXT}}
    - Initial release
    - SICK::Config: the device list (YAML) with the ca, defaults and devices
      sections; unknown keys are an error with their path, type-specific
      keys of a device end up in its options; read_secret reads a secret
      from a file and never puts its content into a message
    - SICK::Bundle: certificate, chain and key as PEM plus what openssl
      reads from the certificate - names (DNS and IP SANs, the CN without
      SANs), validity, SHA-256 fingerprint, subject and issuer DN,
      is_issued_by
    - SICK::Issuer with SICK::Issuer::StepCA (the step CLI against a JWK
      provisioner of step-ca) and SICK::Issuer::SelfSigned (plain openssl,
      for tests and for trying things out)
    - SICK::Target: base class and plugin loader for device types
      ("cisco-sb" loads SICK::Target::CiscoSB), default presented_cert over
      TLS; SICK::Target::Files writes PEM files and runs a reload command
    - SICK::Decision: renew or not - missing, foreign-ca, names, expiring
    - SICK::Runner: check, issue, deploy, check again for every device;
      a device that fails or does not answer does not stop the others, and
      a deployment only counts when the device presents the certificate
      that was issued
    - sick, the command line: check, run (--force, --dry-run, --verbose)
      and trust; exit code 1 when a device failed, 2 for a wrong command
      line or device list
    - SICK::Target::Acmesh: hands the certificate to a deploy hook of
      acme.sh ("type: acmesh"), secrets from files reach the hook through
      its environment only; verified with acme.sh 3.1.6 and its localcopy
      hook
    - SICK::Trust and "sick trust": the root certificate as PEM, DER and
      Apple configuration profile, and a page with the fingerprints and the
      installation steps for each platform
    - "name" in the ca section: what the CA is called on that page
    - X.509 handling goes through the openssl command line, started without
      a shell; runtime dependencies beyond core Perl are Moo (with
      namespace::autoclean and Type::Tiny), Path::Tiny and YAML::PP
    - "intermediates" in the ca section: the intermediate certificate of
      the CA. step-ca signs through an intermediate, and a device that
      presents its certificate alone (a switch with one certificate per
      slot) was "foreign-ca" on every run without it. The root file holds
      the root alone - step refuses a file with two certificates, and so
      does SICK now, saying where the intermediate belongs
    - SICK::Issuer::StepCA verified against step 0.30.2 and step-ca 0.30.2:
      the command lines were right; what step reports is now cleaned of its
      progress lines, colours and debug hint before it reaches the user
    - "foreign-ca" names the issuer readably: fields of a name that hold
      nothing but blanks are left out, no stray backslash
    - docker/: a Compose setup of step-ca (made at the first start, with a
      JWK provisioner for SICK and an ACME provisioner), SICK on a schedule
      and a web server for the page of "sick trust"; t/e2e/run-local.sh
      proves it with Docker alone (a web container as the device),
      t/e2e/run-lab.sh against a real switch; t/e2e/install-tools.sh puts
      step, step-ca and acme.sh in pinned versions into ~/.local
    - docker/README.md: the sick container has no capabilities, so the device
      list and the secrets must be owned by root or readable by everyone
    - The example device list no longer sets ROUTER_OS_PASSWORD, which the
      routeros hook of acme.sh does not read (it logs in with an SSH key);
      the env_files example is the API token of the proxmoxve hook
    - "sick trust" publishes the intermediate certificate as well when the
      ca section names "intermediates": intermediate_ca.pem,
      intermediate_ca.crt (DER) and ca_bundle.pem (root and intermediate in
      one file). A client that installed the root alone kept refusing every
      device that presents its certificate without the intermediate - a
      switch with one certificate per slot. The Apple profile carries the
      root as trusted root and the intermediate as a plain certificate
      (com.apple.security.pkcs1) and replaces the profile of the root alone;
      the page says for each platform where root and intermediate go and
      whether the intermediate takes a trust setting, and has Firefox for
      Android. Without "intermediates" the four files are what they were,
      byte for byte
    - SICK::Trust->write takes intermediates_pem, refuses an intermediate
      that does not lead to the root, and removes the intermediate files of
      an earlier call that it no longer writes; SICK::Trust->files takes
      the number of intermediates
    - t/e2e/run-lab.sh fetches the new files from the trust page and
      connects to the switch with nothing but ca_bundle.pem from there
    - SICK::Issuer, SICK::Issuer::StepCA and SICK::Issuer::SelfSigned are Moo
      classes; an issuer subclass declares its ca keys with has. An empty
      optional key in the ca section counts as not given.
    - SICK::Target, SICK::Target::Files and SICK::Target::Acmesh are Moo
      classes; class_for loads device types with Module::Runtime (new
      prerequisite). Targets written with use parent keep working.
    - SICK::Issuer::SelfSigned no longer moves a new CA key into a directory
      that stands where ca.key belongs; it fails instead.
    - SICK::Runner is a Moo class; now, verify_timeout and verify_interval
      can be read back as attributes.
    - SICK::CLI is a Moo class; the sick command line is unchanged.
    - The trust page no longer tells anyone to put the intermediate
      certificate into the system store of Debian, Fedora or Arch, or into
      Android's CA list. There it became a trust anchor that stayed trusted
      after the root was removed. Those stores get the root only. A device
      that presents its certificate alone is reached through ca_bundle.pem
      as a program's own CA file, or through an intermediate kept without
      trust (Firefox, Chrome on Linux, Windows, macOS, the Apple profile).
      On Android only devices that send their chain are accepted.
    - step-ca: ca.root has to be a single self-signed CA certificate and
      every certificate in ca.intermediates a CA certificate leading to it;
      otherwise each device fails with a message naming the key, the file
      and the reason. Messages say ca.root / ca.intermediates instead of
      "CA root" / "CA intermediates". New SICK::Bundle methods is_ca and
      signs_itself.
    - Docker: docker/ceremony.sh makes root and name-constrained
      intermediate offline and writes a ceremony record; init-ca.sh gains
      an import mode (compose.import.yml) that builds the CA on that
      hierarchy without the root key, the old behaviour is now "lab mode";
      the provisioner "sick" issues serverAuth-only certificates;
      t/e2e/ca-ceremony.sh proves enrollment and path negatives with
      OpenSSL and GnuTLS.
    - A new certificate whose private key does not belong to it is no longer
      deployed: the device fails with "not deployed: the private key does
      not belong to the certificate" before its target is called. New
      SICK::Bundle method key_matches.
    - Docker: docker/ca-backup.sh backs up the CA volume with the CA stopped
      (archive 0600 plus a manifest of checksums and fingerprints) and
      restores it, after checking the manifest, into an empty volume only;
      init-ca.sh --rotate-provisioner replaces key and password of the
      provisioner "sick", hands the password to the sick service and reloads
      step-ca; t/e2e/ca-recovery.sh proves an isolated restore drill and the
      rotation.
    - ca.additional_roots: further root certificates during a root
      replacement (the next root before the switch, the previous one after
      it), checked like ca.root; sick trust publishes them as
      root_ca_2.pem/.crt..., in ca_bundle.pem after the root, in the profile
      as trusted roots, and on the page next to the current root. step and
      the judgement of devices do not use them.
    - sick trust refuses an intermediate that is not a CA certificate
      (CA:TRUE), naming its subject, instead of publishing a device
      certificate of the root as intermediate.
    - Docker: ceremony.sh --renew-intermediate makes a new intermediate under
      the existing root, copying the name constraints from the current one;
      init-ca.sh --switch-intermediate and --retire-intermediate switch a
      running CA with an overlap in which the handover and the trust page
      carry both; docker/compose.ca-next.yml runs the CA of a new root on a
      second volume; t/e2e/ca-rollover.sh proves both with OpenSSL and
      GnuTLS clients.
    - sick trust writes trust-linux.sh, trust-macos.sh, trust-windows.ps1
      and trust-nss.sh, which carry the certificates, install only when
      given the SHA-256 fingerprint of every root (from the CA's operator,
      not the page) and remove exactly what they installed; the page now
      puts that out-of-band check first and adds removal per platform,
      Android's Network Security Configuration limits, the unsigned-profile
      warning and programs with their own trust store.
    - The trust page's section on programs with their own store follows the
      container matrix: requests ignores the system store, Temurin Java and
      Node need their own setup, and loaded roots stay until a restart; each
      line is marked tested in containers or not tried.
    - trust-linux.sh remove on p11-kit systems (Arch) now reports "removed"
      only when an anchor was really there, and "not installed" otherwise,
      like Debian and RHEL; trust anchor --remove exits 0 also when nothing
      matched.
    - sick run keeps one record per device in the state directory (new key
      'state', default /var/lib/sick): last look, last certificate issued
      (serial, fingerprint, names, until, issuer), last deployment, failures
      in a row and back-off; never a key.
    - sick run takes a lock in the state directory; a second run exits with
      the new exit code 3. check, trust and run --dry-run take no lock.
    - A device whose attempt failed after the decision to renew is deferred
      for 1, 2, 4 ... at most 24 hours, less a 15-minute grace (action
      'deferred', exit code 1); --force tries anyway. Discarded certificates
      are not revoked: their key never left a private temporary directory.
    - SICK::Bundle has serial.
    - files: cert.pem, chain.pem, fullchain.pem and key.pem are now links into
      dir/.sick/current, switched to a complete new generation in one step; a
      failed reload goes back to the previous generation and runs the reload
      again; plain files of an earlier version become a generation on the
      first deploy. Mount the directory into containers, not the single
      files.
    - trust-linux.sh: the closing note says that Java from the Debian
      packages follows the system store, while Temurin and other JDKs keep
      their own cacerts (keytool).
    - sick check --format json|prometheus: per device the live certificate
      (fingerprint, serial, issuer, remaining and total lifetime),
      renew_before, the earliest end along its chain to the root, whether it
      is the certificate issued last, and the last success, failures and
      back-off from the records; the root and intermediates of the CA with
      their ends, and whether the CA answers (step ca health).
    - sick check --output FILE replaces the file at once (written beside it,
      mode 0644), for the textfile collector of node_exporter.
    - sick inventory [--format table|json] [--live]: per device the
      certificate issued last, when it was deployed and when a run last saw
      the device present it, from the records alone; --live looks at the
      devices and shows whether each presents it.
    - contrib/prometheus/sick-alerts.yml: alert rules with thresholds
      relative to each device's renew_before, tested with promtool.
    - SICK::Issuer gains health (step-ca: step ca health with a 10-second
      timeout); the states of SICK::Runner->check gain presented.
    - contrib/systemd: sick.service and sick.timer (daily, randomized per
      machine but fixed, catches up after downtime); afterwards always the
      Prometheus textfile and the trust page; on failure
      sick-notify@.service; exit code 3 (lock held) is no failure.
    - contrib/notify/sick-notify: sends the failed run to a webhook (URL
      from a file, never in an argument or the output) and/or by mail,
      configured in /etc/sick/notify.conf.
    - contrib/cron/sick: a cron.d line that notifies on failure except for
      exit code 3, writes the textfile and the trust page.
    - k8s/cronjob: sick run as a CronJob (Forbid, no retries), metrics
      served for Prometheus, alert rules for failed Jobs.
    - t/e2e/ops.sh: container tests for the notifications, the alert rules
      against real output of sick check (stale device, unreachable CA), and
      the CronJob in a k3d cluster.
    - The CA's ACME provisioner "acme" now issues server certificates with
      serverAuth only, for 30 days by default and at most 90. Set the
      lifetimes with SICK_CA_ACME_LIFETIME, SICK_CA_ACME_MAX_LIFETIME and
      SICK_CA_ACME_MIN_LIFETIME; they are applied at every start and checked
      as Go durations with min <= lifetime <= max.
    - contrib/acme: recipes for Traefik, Caddy and nginx with acme.sh against
      step-ca's ACME endpoint, each with root trust for the CA connection,
      renewal tuned to 30-day certificates and its enrollment authorization
      written down; t/e2e/acme-services.sh shows each obtains, renews with
      the fingerprint on the port changing, is refused a name outside the CA
      policy and fails a challenge for a name it does not control.
